{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1227839-a-question-the-pound-of-coins","id":1227839,"title":"A question the pound of coins","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-06-16T03:49:00-07:00","id":1227839,"title":"A question the pound of coins","content":"Is the pound used to weight silver and jade based on anything or is it purely &quot;in universe&quot;?<br /> <br /> there is no 64 pound talent that I have found in any historical measurement sistem that used pounds, minae, obols, even shekels where I looked in The Book that mentions them most<br /> <br /> so I couldn't convert it to anything Imperial, Troy or metric or compare them to coins of similar worth when they were at their widest use<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12198-alexanderbk","name":"alexanderbk","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"38"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-19T18:05:02-07:00","id":1228717,"title":"","content":"There's no reference in 1e core, Manacle and Coin, 2e core, Masters of Jade, or 3e core of what pound is being used to measure currency, so the logical assumption (and the one that would be most compatible with the rest of the game) is that it is the same pound used for other weights in the game books. Which is also never specifically declared, but is presumably the standard pound currently used in the United States (the international pound avoirdupois of 453.59237 grams established in 1959), or something close enough that Americans using their intuition of what a &quot;pound&quot; is won't be led astray.<br /> <br /> In that case the dinar, at 0.04 pounds avoirdupois, is 0.64 ounces avoirdupois, 0.583\u0305 ounces troy, 280 grains, or 18.1436948 grams (at full precision), a passable fit for the Manacle and Coin claim of the dinar being a &quot;half-ounce&quot;. Silver purity of Guild-struck coins was specified in Manacle &amp; Coin as 97% (which is weirdly pure for something intended to circulate).<br /> <br /> (If we instead assume that the M&amp;C &quot;half ounce&quot; specifically means 0.50000 international ounces avoirdupois, then the dinar is 14.1747616 grams, the &quot;game silver pound&quot; which 3e establishes is 25 times the weight of the dinar is 354.369039 grams, the 3e dirham is 5.66990462 kilograms, and the 3e silver talent is 22.6796185 kilograms. This is a weird back-formation from a 1e incidental that I would discourage, but for the purpose of comparing to historic coins doesn't actually cause all that much difference, because historical fluctuations in the valuation of silver over time and geography, changes in weight and purity of coins in circulation, and other confounders involving historical price lists swamp a &quot;mere&quot; 22% difference in weight.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-21T00:45:57-07:00","id":1229109,"title":"","content":"my idea was they might be using troy pounds (heavier ounce but only 12 ounces in the pound) since it mostly used for precious metals, or some undisclosed Realm Pound,<br /> Think I might check some chinese measures see if any seems &quot;poundish&quot; since the cash system is obviously Asian inspired, well the jade system is inspired by classical Greece and Middle East","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12198-alexanderbk","name":"alexanderbk","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"38"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-21T19:49:51-07:00","id":1229279,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>alexanderbk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1227839-a-question-the-pound-of-coins?p=1229109#post1229109\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">my idea was they might be using troy pounds (heavier ounce but only 12 ounces in the pound) since it mostly used for precious metals, or some undisclosed Realm Pound,<br /> Think I might check some chinese measures see if any seems &quot;poundish&quot; since the cash system is obviously Asian inspired, well the jade system is inspired by classical Greece and Middle East</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, troy-because-precious is a perfectly plausible interpretation, it just doesn't seem to be supported by anything explicitly stated anywhere.<br /> <br /> The obvious Chinese cultural area &quot;pound&quot;, which is still frequently used for gold and silver exchange, is the catty (keti, jin, \u65a4), which has a number of different modern standardizations -- 604.78982 grams in Hong Kong, 604.79 grams in Malaysia, 604.8 grams in Singapore, 600 grams in Taiwan, Japan, Korea, and Thailand. It's divided into 16 tael (tahil, liang, \u4e24, \u5169), which are thus obvious &quot;ounce&quot; equivalents. In that case (using the rounded-off one just so I can do the math in my head), you'd have a 38.4 kilo silver talent, a 9.6 kilo silver dirham, and a 24-gram (0.64 tael &quot;ounce&quot;) silver dinar. That's roughly a third-again heavier than a pound avoirdupois, which is close enough that it shouldn't cause any serious problems with intuitions when interacting with Charms that use pounds as a unit of measure.<br /> <br /> The modern mainland Chinese 500 gram version of the catty divided into ten 50 gram tael is so blatantly metricized that it doesn't seem to carry any of the flavor of using a &quot;Chinese measure&quot; to me. but the numbers would be 32 kilos for the silver talent, 8 kilos for the dirham, and a 20-gram (and thus 0.4 new tael / &quot;ounce&quot;) dinar. It is of course much closer to a pound avoirdupois.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}